`mech format`
===============================================================================

`mech format` formats Mech source and Mechdown documents.

1. Format source
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

```sh
mech format main.mec --out formatted
```

2. Format multiple inputs
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

```sh
mech format src/main.mec docs/index.mec --out formatted
```

3. HTML docs generation
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

`mech format --html` renders a standalone Mech source document through the
selected HTML shim. The shim owns the page shell, while known Mech slots are
expanded from the document in one non-recursive pass.

For a blog post:

```sh
mech format post.mec \
  --html \
  --shim include/blog.html \
  --stylesheet include/blog.css \
  --out post.html
```

For a documentation page:

```sh
mech format docs/page.mec \
  --html \
  --shim include/docs.html \
  --stylesheet include/docs.css \
  --out page.html
```

The shipped shims opt into the shared browser controller through
`{{DOCUMENT_SCRIPT}}`. For a controller-backed document, formatting also writes
`_mech/pkg/mech_wasm.js` and `_mech/pkg/mech_wasm_bg.wasm` next to the output
tree and embeds a base64 source bundle for the root and every resolved relative
Mech dependency. Each generated HTML page refers to the package with a relative
module URL, so deploy the HTML tree and `_mech/` together through an ordinary
static HTTP server. Standalone documents require neither `mech.mcfg`,
`project.js`, nor `mech serve` routes.

A custom shim without `{{DOCUMENT_SCRIPT}}` remains intentionally static and
does not cause a runtime package to be emitted. A custom shim that opts into
the shared controller must provide `{{WASM_MODULE_URL}}` and
`{{DOCUMENT_SOURCES}}` so its output remains deployable without the server.
